## Test Smell Audit — Academic 19-Smell Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`  
**Framework:** MSTest 4.x / .NET 10  
**Integration calibration:** Applied — class has `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)  
**Academic name:** *Sleepy Test* (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // 3 seconds of dead time per run
```

**Integration calibration note:** Even for integration tests, hard sleeps are a smell — they cause non-deterministic failures and inflate suite runtime. A polling/retry pattern or event-driven signal is appropriate.

**Fix:**
```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; suite slows linearly as sleep-based tests accumulate.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)  
**Academic name:** *Conditional Test Logic* (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** Only one branch ever executes — the other is dead code giving false coverage confidence. The test never verifies which type was actually returned.

**Fix:** Write two explicit tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    // arrange standard user
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasDiscountRate()
{
    // arrange premium user
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("premium@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden test gap — you don't know which branch actually runs, so one scenario is permanently untested.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)  
**Academic name:** *Unknown Test / Empty Test* (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Problem:** Passes even if Insert silently drops every row. The name implies "no exception = success" but MSTest already fails on unhandled exceptions — this test adds no value beyond what the framework gives for free.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage reports show this code as "tested" but nothing is actually verified.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)  
**Academic name:** *Assertion Roulette* (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions without message parameters. When one fails in CI, the test output only shows a bare failure — you must read the source to determine which property was wrong.

**Fix:** Add message parameters:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slow triage when tests break — especially painful in CI logs.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-structured:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single action, clear null-assertion, minimal setup |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count is self-documenting (3 items added → assert 3); uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Exercises concurrency edge case with clear postcondition |
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Focused arrange-act-assert with meaningful verification |

The setup/teardown is also clean — in-memory SQLite provides full isolation without Mystery Guest concerns.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 2 min | Eliminates a false-pass test |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 5 min | Closes a hidden test gap |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 10 min | Removes flakiness + 3s from suite |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 3 min | Improves CI debuggability |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests are clean. The 3 high-severity findings are the ones your team should prioritize — they represent real gaps in verification rather than stylistic preferences.
